Current:
```
#!/usr/bin/env node
const arg = process.argv[2];
if (arg === "--upper") console.log((process.argv[3] || "").toUpperCase());
else { console.error("usage: cli.js --upper <text>"); process.exit(1); }
```

New behavior. Requirements:
- `shout --upper --repeat 3 hello` prints "HELLO" three times.
- `shout --upper hello` prints HELLO once (default repeat 1).
- `--repeat 1` → 1 line.
- `--repeat 0`, negative, non-numeric → sensible error (non-zero exit).
- `--repeat banana` → non-zero exit (checker).
- N=3 → 3 HI lines, exit 0.

Arg parsing: flags can come in any order? Card example: `shout --upper --repeat 3 hello`. So --upper and --repeat 3 precede the text. Let me write a small arg parser that scans argv[2..] for `--upper`, `--repeat <N>`, and positional text.

Design:
```js
#!/usr/bin/env node
const args = process.argv.slice(2);
let upper = false, repeat = 1, text = null;

for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
  const a = args[i];
  if (a === "--upper") { upper = true; }
  else if (a === "--repeat") {
    const n = args[++i];
    if (n === undefined || !/^-?\d+$/.test(n)) {
      console.error("usage: cli.js --upper [--repeat N] <text>");
      process.exit(1);
    }
    repeat = Number(n);
  }
  else if (a.startsWith("--")) {
    console.error("unknown flag: " + a);
    process.exit(1);
  }
  else { text = a; }
}

if (text === undefined) {
  console.error("usage: cli.js --upper [--repeat N] <text>");
  process.exit(1);
}
if (repeat < 1) {
  console.error("--repeat N requires N >= 1 (got " + repeat + ")");
  process.exit(1);
}

const out = upper ? text.toUpperCase() : text;
for (let i = 0; i < repeat; i++) console.log(out);
```

Wait — checker: `node cli.js --upper --repeat banana hi` must exit non-zero. With my parse: --repeat then n="banana" fails `/^-?\d+$/` → exit 1. Good. But "banana" is consumed as the repeat value, so text would be "hi". Fine, we exit before that.

Check checker: `--repeat 3 hi` → repeat=3, text="hi" → prints "HI" 3x, grep -c HI = 3. Good (upper).
`--repeat 1 hi` → 1 line HI. Good.
`--repeat banana hi`: non-zero. Good.

Edge: what if text value could be confused with flag? Not relevant.

One concern: the negative input `-3`. My regex `^-?\d+$` matches "-3", then repeat<1 → error exit. Good. Non-numeric "banana" → regex fails → exit 1. N=0 → repeat<1 error. Good.

Now, note the checker greps for "HI". Output lines must contain "HI" (uppercase). Since text is "hi" and upper applied → "HI". Good.

Also keep backward compat: `--upper hello` prints HELLO. Fine.

